#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084129} :activity/id #uuid "75327081-f3af-5627-aeee-e63e6626e61b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050440 :topic/id #uuid "4946f4a6-450a-568b-b2a2-aa465119c958" :topic/math-academy-id 736} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084453 :tutorial/content "A common problem in statistics is recognizing whether data follows a trend. Thankfully, we can often identify trends in data simply by looking at a scatter plot. We list a few common examples below:\n\n- **Positive linear correlation**: The points seem to follow a straight line with a positive slope.\n\n![](images/e5/e5b155a661e5cb548c30cc34af0933087a4b63b63295b4667757c355d1a32802.png)\n\n- **Negative linear correlation**: The points seem to follow a straight line with a negative slope.\n\n![](images/3d/3dc0506d5686bbc583a86960343a6d4019071edb45121a9688d4b482f16ac8f8.png)\n\n- **Quadratic correlation**: The points on the plot are scattered along a parabola.\n\n![](images/53/53ed6cecb371e0f31623803568d6e1d97057652f13156da9cc71a64f91beadb3.png)\n\n- **Exponential correlation**: The points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve.\n\n![](images/29/2976d466ec40ff595f15f78b0a90e0f0ecc34c8c43c4f10179b7f124f73330f9.png)\n\n- **No correlation**: The points seem to be scattered randomly.\n\n![](images/eb/eb025d0075bf8c8f6ac236759fa786b3c7f145a8187e8c219f7fe0ee82d3a0f7.png)\n\nThe process of fitting data to a linear, quadratic, exponential, or another type of curve is called **regression**." :tutorial/id #uuid "41441cda-b0e7-53e7-ad07-57f8c12fdcfb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3704 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0db20ae8-4b57-5c01-a3c9-b037ecc1f9c3" :step/math-academy-id 20636 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084130}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083028 :question/id #uuid "0ac76a89-dd8b-5f79-96ee-b73f96d98c87" :question/math-academy-id "e-5718"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1aa6320-1643-5363-9ccc-8b86b48a9609" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049976}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233106}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Appropriate Regression Model"} :step/id #uuid "e9396b63-3259-5e91-b33f-bc8207544992" :step/math-academy-id 20637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084131}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083029 :question/id #uuid "b3c9f5da-2d02-5d4d-b519-818b77320211" :question/math-academy-id "e-5719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9100136d-6adb-5138-bab2-55c278876811" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083570}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Diagram Corresponding to a Model"} :step/id #uuid "0c6689f9-79f9-5e96-a3d8-4fa412cfbac6" :step/math-academy-id 20638}] :activity/title "Selecting a Regression Model" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083429}]}
